WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.170
Our school keeps the class teachers' phone

2
00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:05.170
numbers in eight different sheets.

3
00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.410
One teacher got a new number last month.

4
00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:10.360
Guess how many sheets

5
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:11.880
still have the old one?

6
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.880
Today: from scattered sheets to a

7
00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:19.400
database, where every fact lives in one

8
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:19.740
place.

9
00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.870
A database is a set of tables kept by

10
00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:26.050
one program, a database system.

11
00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.740
Everyone works on the same copy.

12
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.520
Each column has a type, like

13
00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.300
whole numbers or text.

14
00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.250
And rules stop bad data getting in.

15
00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:40.470
The big idea: store each fact once.

16
00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.020
The teacher's phone belongs with the

17
00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.440
class, not in every pupil's row.

18
00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.030
Then a change happens in one

19
00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.590
place, and every report sees it.

20
00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.430
Each table has a primary key: a

21
00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:56.190
column that picks out one row,

22
00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:57.470
like the pupil's number.

23
00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.000
Another table points to it with

24
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.240
a foreign key: each mark row

25
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:03.620
holds the pupil's number.

26
00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:06.450
Keys are how tables link up.

27
00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:09.810
You talk to a database in SQL.

28
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.080
CREATE TABLE describes a table: its

29
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.780
columns, their types and its key.

30
00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.920
Then SELECT asks for rows back.

31
00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.390
Think of a school office with one

32
00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.770
filing cabinet, instead of every

33
00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:26.010
teacher keeping a copy.

34
00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.860
One drawer for pupils, one for

35
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.560
classes, one for marks.

36
00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:33.380
Each file has a number, and

37
00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:34.780
other files refer to it.

38
00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:36.430
Quick check!

39
00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.060
In a well-designed school

40
00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.660
database, where should a class

41
00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:43.640
teacher's phone number be stored?

42
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:46.530
Once, in the classes table.

43
00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:49.490
Each pupil's row just says which

44
00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:51.690
class they're in, and the class row

45
00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:53.530
holds the teacher and the phone.

46
00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:55.980
Change it once, and every

47
00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:57.500
pupil's report is right.

48
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.480
What goes wrong without it?

49
00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:03.310
Update the phone in one row, miss the

50
00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:05.290
others, and the data gives two numbers.

51
00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:08.890
Delete the last pupils of a class, and the

52
00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:10.730
teacher's phone disappears with them.

53
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:14.640
In the app, split one wide school

54
00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.360
table into two tables, then three.

55
00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.510
Watch the repeated facts fall from

56
00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:24.410
thirty-two to eight to zero, and try a

57
00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.230
hurried change on each design.

58
00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:28.310
Your turn.

59
00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:32.090
Each mark row stores the pupil's number,

60
00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:35.150
which points to a row in the pupils table.

61
00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.480
What is that column called?

62
00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:41.090
A foreign key: it points to a

63
00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:42.210
row in another table.

64
00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:45.370
Remember: a database keeps one

65
00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:48.450
shared copy, with types and rules.

66
00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:52.730
Store each fact once, in its own table.

67
00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:56.110
And keys, primary and foreign,

68
00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:57.830
link the tables together.

69
00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:02.270
Try it in class tomorrow: draw one wide

70
00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:05.410
table on the board, with pupils, their

71
00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:08.090
class, the class teacher and her phone.

72
00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:10.350
Pupils split it into two

73
00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:11.630
tables, with a key.

74
00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:14.600
Then change the phone number: how

75
00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:16.420
many places did each design need?
